    "body": "Follows #340 and #341. `gh-pr-pdf` fetched every pull request again on every run. After the first run it now asks `github-backup` for only what changed.\n\n## Changes\nAll in `gh-pr-pdf/` (script and `README.org`, which has a new \"Running it again\" section).\n\n- Fetching: the script always passes `github-backup -i`. The first run has no checkpoints, so it fetches everything and `github-backup` writes them. Later runs request only new data since the last successful backup.\n- `backup/.gh-pr-pdf-state.json` records the last full fetch, the options it used and a hash per rendered pull request. A failed fetch does not move it forward.\n- A full fetch is forced:\n  - when no earlier one is recorded, or with `--full`;\n  - when the options differ from the last run, because `-i` does not backfill a flag that is new;\n  - when the last full fetch is older than `--full-every DAYS` (30 by default, 0 turns it off). The `github-backup` documentation warns that a failed earlier run can leave a gap in an incremental backup; this is the cure.\n  - A full fetch first deletes `github-backup`'s own checkpoint files (`last_update`, `reviews_last_update`) in `backup/`, so it is not cut short by an older checkpoint.\n- Rendering: an org file is rebuilt only when the JSON of its pull request changed (or the renderer did), since `pandoc` runs several times per pull request. Org files of pull requests that vanish from the backup are removed. The PDF is still built from all the org files, a local step that does not use the API.\n- Discussions stay a full fetch each time (one query per 25 discussions).\n- The progress line says which mode ran and why, for example `fetching pull requests: incremental (last full fetch 3 days ago, ...)`.\n\n## Not run\n`github-backup` is not installed in the container and there is no way to install it, so the incremental mode was run against a stand-in that logs how it is called and writes checkpoint files the way its documentation describes. Covered: first run, an unchanged rerun (0 rendered, 3 unchanged), one changed pull request (1 rendered), `--full` (checkpoints deleted first), changed options, a 40-day-old state against 29 days, `--full-every 0`, a failed fetch (state unchanged), a pull request that vanishes, and a corrupt state file.\n\nNot seen, and worth checking on a real run:\n- How `github-backup` applies `-i` to pull requests, and whether it fetches the comments and commits of one that changed (the PyPI page only says that it requests new data since the last successful backup).\n- That it fetches everything when it finds no checkpoint, which the first run and every full fetch rely on.\n- Quick check: compare the API requests of the second run with the first (`gh api rate_limit` before and after).\n\n`typst compile` is still not run in the container either (see #341).\n\nNo CHANGELOG edit, per the nightly-tidy rule.\n\n---\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_01WWLNi91AWH6eASFA8xAktp)_",
